---
import Film from '../../plate/Film.astro';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './style.css';

const labels = [
  { id: 'nucleus-charge', tex: String.raw`Z` },
  { id: 'electron-count', tex: String.raw`n` },
  { id: 'energy-axis', tex: String.raw`E_n` },
  { id: 'binding', tex: String.raw`E_n<E_{n-1}` },
  { id: 'plateau', tex: String.raw`E_n=E_{n-1}` },
  { id: 'last-binding', tex: String.raw`N_c` },
  { id: 'lieb-wall', tex: String.raw`2Z+1` },
  { id: 'lieb-molecular-wall', tex: String.raw`2Z+M` },
  { id: 'lieb-at-most', tex: String.raw`N_c\le2Z` },
  { id: 'symbolic-atom', tex: String.raw`Z+C` },
  { id: 'symbolic-molecule', tex: String.raw`Z+CM` },
  { id: 'total-charge', tex: String.raw`Z=\sum_{j=1}^{M}z_j,\quad z_j\ge1` },
] as const;
---

<Film
  tag="uruk-excess-stairs"
  cues={CUES}
  timeline={TIMELINE}
  step={50}
  labels={labels}
  description="A nucleus and a row of electron slots carry a question about strict binding into a schematic ground-state energy staircase. Downward steps mean that adding an electron lowers the energy, while a flat continuation means no strict binding. Every step height, displayed count and plateau position is labelled schematic. Lieb's exact exclusion wall is shown separately with the integer-charge values three, five, seven and nine. A symbolic bracket asks whether the excess can be universally bounded. The bracket alone turns blue for the paper's claim, then extends to a system of fixed nuclei with total charge Z. Its width has no numerical meaning. The closing ledger preserves the dated unreviewed status and distinguishes formal statements for the two companions from the headline theorem."
  caption="The staircase illustrates strict binding and an eventual energy plateau. Its heights, step counts and stopping position are schematic, not a computed atom. Lieb's wall values are exact for integer charges one through four. The blue bracket states the paper's claim for the full nonrelativistic Schrödinger model with two electron spin states and fixed nuclei of charges at least one: at most Z plus C times the number of nuclei are strictly bound, with Z the total charge. The universal constant C is not computed; the bracket is symbolic and its width is not a number."
  leftOut="Computed atomic energies or densities; the periodic table and first-ionization data; the outer half-electron radius; the history of bounds beyond Lieb's; the screening and conditioning proof; numerical values of the claimed universal constants."
>
  <p class="film-method">How the film is made. The nucleus, electron slots and staircase illustrate definitions from the article. No atom, wavefunction, density or energy is computed. Every drawn step height, the number of generic steps and the position marked <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`N_c`, false)} /> are schematic. Changing the nucleus label does not turn the drawn stair into the binding sequence for that charge. The laboratory scene reports the article's observation about isolated atoms; its tiny step and crossed slot are illustrations, not a calculation or a mathematical exclusion theorem.</p>
  <p class="film-method">A downward step represents <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`E_n<E_{n-1}`, false)} />, so the added electron is strictly bound. A flat continuation represents equality, and the sequence never rises. The pictured last drop carries the definition of the largest strictly binding number, not a measured or calculated excess. The claim concerns strict binding and does not exclude a ground state exactly at threshold.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The browser computes only Lieb's exclusion values <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`2Z+1=3,5,7,9`, false)} /> at the integer charges <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`Z=1,2,3,4`, false)} />. For these integer charges, exclusion of <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`2Z+1`, false)} /> leaves at most <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`2Z`, false)} /> strictly binding electrons, hence room for <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`Z`, false)} /> extra. When the molecular picture introduces more nuclei, the retained known exclusion wall changes from the atom's <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`2Z+1`, false)} /> to <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`2Z+M`, false)} />. The schematic staircase does not supply a tighter bound or identify a real atom's last bound electron.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The question bracket and blue claimed bracket are symbolic marks, not intervals measured on the electron-count axis. Their widths do not give a value of <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`C`, false)} />, compare it with <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`Z`, false)} /> or compare the new bound geometrically with Lieb's wall. The single-nucleus form is <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`N_c\le Z+C`, false)} />. The claimed molecular form is <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`N_c\le Z+CM`, false)} /> for <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`M`, false)} /> fixed point nuclei with charges <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`z_j\ge1`, false)} /> and total charge <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`Z=\sum_jz_j`, false)} />. The nuclei occupy distinct positions, which may be arbitrarily close or far apart. The universal constant is independent of their charges and positions and has not been computed.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The headline claim uses the full nonrelativistic Schrödinger model, antisymmetric electron states with two spin states, fixed nuclei and no magnetic field. It is not a Hartree-Fock or Thomas-Fermi computation. Blue is used only for the claimed symbolic bracket. Labels, formulas, the laboratory picture and Lieb's wall stay in ink. The schematic molecular extension introduces no electron-count prediction for a particular arrangement of nuclei.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The closing ledger preserves the article's unreviewed status and evidence date, 20 October 2026. The pinned release's scope note covers the two companion statements only. <code>CoulombIonization.lean</code>, the generalized-ionization asymptotics, is catalogued. <code>CoulombRadii.lean</code>, the outer-radius asymptotics, is linked but not catalogued. The headline excess-charge theorem, first-ionization bounds and half-electron-radius bounds have no comparator statement in that release. The dated search found no outside replay; the film performs none. A formal statement or kernel check does not count as reading or approving the papers.</p>
  <p class="film-method">With JavaScript disabled, the article and transcript remain available without a fallback illustration. Reduced motion selects a completed supporting frame for each of the ten exact canonical cues. The clauses cut in the Lieb and Simon subtitles follow the direction's documented sentence forms.</p>
</Film>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
